WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:31.640
<v Emanuele Berry>From WBEZ Chicago, It's This American Life. I'm Emanuele Berry in for Ira Glass. So today's show starts with an email that was sent in the summer of 2020, from a man named Dr. James Whitfield. And if you remember that summer, everyone was sending out emails and tweets about race and racism in America, statements of unity from corporations. One shoe brand tweeted, "We are not asking you to buy our shoes. We are asking you to walk in someone else's."
<v Emanuele Berry>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《美国生活》。我是Emanuele Berry，代班Ira Glass。今天的节目始于2020年夏天的一封邮件，发件人是一位名叫James Whitfield博士的男士。如果你还记得那个夏天，当时每个人都在发送关于美国种族和种族主义的电子邮件和推文，企业也纷纷发表团结声明。一家鞋类品牌发推称：“我们不是要求你买我们的鞋，我们是想让你换位思考，体验别人的生活。”

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:47.120
<v Emanuele Berry>Remember when everyone on Instagram posted black squares for a day to show solidarity with the Black community? I'd started to roll my eyes at all the MLK and Baldwin quotes. The murder of George Floyd had forced the country into another racial awakening.
<v Emanuele Berry>还记得Instagram上每个人都发布了一天的黑色方块，以示对黑人社区的声援吗？我对所有关于马丁·路德·金和鲍德温的引言开始感到厌倦。乔治·弗洛伊德的谋杀案迫使整个国家进行了又一次种族觉醒。

00:00:47.120 --> 00:01:02.340
<v Emanuele Berry>In a school district outside Dallas, Texas, Dr. James Whitfield had just been promoted to high school principal, the school's first Black principal. And he was watching everyone send out these emails, not just corporate brands, but also his peers, other educators and administrators.
<v Emanuele Berry>在德克萨斯州达拉斯市外的一个学区，James Whitfield博士刚被提升为高中校长，成为该校首位黑人校长。他看着每个人都在发送这些电子邮件，不仅是企业品牌，还有他的同行、其他教育工作者和行政人员。

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:11.300
<v James Whitfield>I had been up pretty much all night, could not sleep. And I woke up at 4:30 in the morning. And I said, I have to craft something.
<v James Whitfield>我几乎彻夜未眠。早上4点半醒来时，我说，我必须写点什么。

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:38.660
<v Emanuele Berry>His email started by talking about the killings of George Floyd, Ahmaud Arbery, and Breonna Taylor, and how these events had brought forth the familiar enemy of racism in America. Quote, "For so long these atrocities have occurred, and we've simply moved on with our daily lives. Now it appears as though we are collectively using our voice to denounce systemic racism and the inequities that people of color face on a daily basis in our country."
<v Emanuele Berry>他的邮件开头谈到了乔治·弗洛伊德、艾哈迈德·阿伯里和布里安娜·泰勒的遇害，以及这些事件如何让美国种族主义这个熟悉的敌人再次浮出水面。他写道：“长期以来，这些暴行一直在发生，而我们只是继续过着我们的日常生活。现在看来，我们正共同发声，谴责系统性种族主义以及有色人种在我国每天面临的不平等。”

00:01:38.660 --> 00:02:05.450
<v Emanuele Berry>He goes on to write from a personal perspective of a Black man who grew up in Texas. He writes, I will be 42 years old next month. And never in my life have I experienced this level of support when it comes to issues of race. I cannot begin to tell you how encouraging it has been to have so many of my white brothers and sisters buck the status quo by calling, texting, unashamedly saying that Black Lives do indeed matter. He continued--
<v Emanuele Berry>他接着从一名在德克萨斯州长大的黑人男性的个人视角出发。他写道，下个月我就42岁了。在我的一生中，从未在种族问题上感受到如此程度的支持。我无法形容当看到我这么多白人兄弟姐妹通过打电话、发短信、毫不避讳地说出“黑人的命也是命”来挑战现状时，我感到多么受鼓舞。他继续写道——

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:13.000
<v James Whitfield>I'm here with you to do whatever we need to do to disrupt systemic racism and eradicate it.
<v James Whitfield>我愿与你们同在，共同采取我们需要采取的一切行动，去瓦解并根除系统性种族主义。

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:44.510
<v Emanuele Berry>Whitfield ends his email the way he ends many of his emails and messages, by telling people he loves them dearly, which is kind of who he is, approachable, warm, a beloved figure, a cardigan-wearing dad. People appreciated the email. Parents, teachers, and students wrote to say thank you. Some said they were ready to learn more. One parent mentioned how refreshing it was to see a school leader send out this kind of letter.
<v Emanuele Berry>Whitfield以他结尾许多邮件和信息的方式结束了这封信，告诉大家他深爱着他们。这就是他的为人：平易近人、温暖、受人爱戴，一位穿着开衫的父亲。人们很赞赏这封邮件。家长、老师和学生写信表示感谢。有人说他们准备好了解更多了。一位家长提到，看到一位学校领导发出这样的信是多么令人耳目一新。

00:02:44.510 --> 00:03:11.790
<v Emanuele Berry>A year passed, Whitfield's first year as principal. And it's a tough one because, you know, the pandemic. But he made it through. And then he arrived at the summer of 2021. It is a very different landscape from the summer of 2020. In fact, the script has flipped. Public conversations have moved from let's all try and understand and talk about systemic racism to let's never mention systemic racism.
<v Emanuele Berry>一年过去了，这是Whitfield担任校长的第一年。因为疫情，这一年很艰难。但他挺过来了。接着到了2021年的夏天。与2020年夏天相比，环境截然不同。事实上，剧本翻转了。公众讨论从“让我们努力理解并谈论系统性种族主义”变成了“绝不要提及系统性种族主义”。

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:40.430
<v Emanuele Berry>This is especially true in Texas, where Dr. Whitfield is. In Texas, the conversation is suddenly all about banning critical race theory. Critical Race Theory, CRT, you've probably heard about it. It's a way to chart how racism is ingrained in the American legal system and other institutions. But at this moment, CRT has become kind of a boogeyman, a quick shorthand to shut down anything acknowledging racism or even Blackness.
<v Emanuele Berry>在Whitfield博士所在的德克萨斯州尤其如此。在德克萨斯州，对话突然全部变成了禁止批判性种族理论（CRT）。你可能听说过CRT。这是一种揭示种族主义如何根植于美国法律制度及其他机构的方式。但在此时，CRT已成为一种“稻草人”，成为打压任何承认种族主义甚至黑人身份的快速借口。

00:03:40.430 --> 00:04:09.460
<v Emanuele Berry>Texas passed a law in 2021 banning CRT in schools. And during the school year, Dr. Whitfield heard that some people were grumbling about him on social media, saying he was a race warrior. The online grumblings became public at a school board meeting in July, when a resident points to Whitfield's email, his email where he basically says it seems like we're ready to talk about race as a country. He points to that email as proof that he is indoctrinating students with critical race theory.
<v Emanuele Berry>德克萨斯州在2021年通过了一项法律，禁止在学校传授CRT。在那个学年里，Whitfield博士听说有人在社交媒体上抱怨他，说他是“种族战士”。这种网络抱怨在7月的校董会会议上公开了，当时一名居民指向Whitfield的邮件——那封他基本上表达了“作为国家我们似乎准备好谈论种族问题了”的邮件——将其作为他正在向学生灌输批判性种族理论的证据。

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:30.860
<v Mr. Clark>Tonight I would like to express my concerns, not only of myself, but of many in our community, about the implementation of critical race theory in our district, specifically the views and goals of the principal of Colleyville Heritage High School, James Whitfield. I was first made aware of Mr Whitfield's extreme views on race when a concerned friend of mine shared with me a letter he sent to parents and students in the summer of 2020.
<v Mr. Clark>今晚，我不仅代表我自己，也代表社区中的许多人，表达我们对本学区实施批判性种族理论的担忧，特别是关于Colleyville Heritage高中校长James Whitfield的观点和目标。我是通过一位关心此事的友人在2020年夏天分享给我的一封他发给家长和学生的信，才了解到Whitfield先生在种族问题上的极端观点。

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:49.670
<v Emanuele Berry>To be clear, Dr. Whitfield is not teaching CRT. He didn't propose educational reforms in his email. He wasn't reshaping the curriculum. He did support an existing program at the school that tried to get kids into college who wouldn't traditionally go. He got flak for that. But the program predated him.
<v Emanuele Berry>需要明确的是，Whitfield博士并没有教授CRT。他在邮件中没有提出教育改革建议。他也没有重塑课程。他确实支持学校内一个现有的旨在帮助那些传统上无法进入大学的孩子升学的项目。他因此受到了抨击。但该项目在他任职前就已经存在了。

00:04:49.670 --> 00:05:08.800
<v Emanuele Berry>People complained about an approving mention of the Southern Poverty Law Center, but also that he quoted Gil Scott-Heron in an email saying, "The revolution will not be televised." And, of course, that email from 2020 where, like everyone else in 2020, he denounces systemic racism.
<v Emanuele Berry>人们抱怨他赞许地提到了南方贫困法律中心，还因为他在邮件中引用了吉尔·斯科特-赫伦的话说“革命不会被电视转播”。当然，还有那封2020年的邮件，在那封信里，像2020年的其他人一样，他谴责了系统性种族主义。

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.940
<v Mr. Clark>Later in this letter, he goes further.
<v Mr. Clark>在这封信的后面，他进一步写道。

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.990
<v Board Member 1>Mr. [INAUDIBLE], Mr. Clark.
<v Board Member 1>[听不清]先生，Clark先生。

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:13.470
<v Mr. Clark>Yes.
<v Mr. Clark>是。

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:18.350
<v Board Member 1>We really prefer that you don't criticize a particular employee of the district.
<v Board Member 1>我们真的希望您不要批评本学区的特定员工。

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:18.950
<v Mr. Clark>OK.
<v Mr. Clark>好的。

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:20.330
<v Board Member 1>If you have any issues, we--
<v Board Member 1>如果你有什么问题，我们……

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:23.000
<v Man 1>How about you fire that clown? How's that--
<v Man 1>你干脆开除那个小丑怎么样？

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:23.990
<v Board Member 1>Sir--
<v Board Member 1>先生……

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:24.540
<v Man 2>Mr.--
<v Man 2>先生……

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:25.370
<v Man 1>How about you fire that--
<v Man 1>开除那个……怎么样？

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:26.050
<v Woman 1>Hey, keep it down!
<v Woman 1>嘿，安静点！

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:26.680
<v Board Member 1>Sir.
<v Board Member 1>先生。

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:46.850
<v Emanuele Berry>At the time, it seemed absurd that this would actually happen, that Dr. Whitfield would be fired. A handful of people at a board meeting demanding a respected and newly hired principal be fired for promoting an academic theory he wasn't promoting, that seemed hard to imagine. But over the next few months, that is what happened.
<v Emanuele Berry>当时，这看起来荒谬至极，Whitfield博士竟然真的会被解雇。在校董会上，几个人要求解雇一位受人尊敬且新聘的校长，理由是他推广了一项他并未推广的学术理论，这似乎很难想象。但在接下来的几个月里，事情真的就这样发生了。

00:05:46.850 --> 00:06:09.070
<v Emanuele Berry>In August, the board placed Dr. Whitfield on paid administrative leave but didn't give a reason why. He talked to the media about it. In September, Dr. Whitfield defended himself at a board meeting. One of the items on the agenda was should the district renew Whitfield's contract for the next year. Whitfield showed up for the public comments and, like everyone else, he got one minute to speak.
<v Emanuele Berry>8月，校董会让Whitfield博士带薪行政休假，但没有给出原因。他向媒体谈到了这件事。9月，Whitfield博士在校董会上为自己辩护。会议议程之一是学区是否应续签Whitfield下一年的合同。Whitfield出席了公众意见环节，和所有人一样，他有一分钟发言时间。

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:27.240
<v James Whitfield>Hello, I'm Dr. James Whitfield. Dr. Ryan, board of trustees, I first want to express my gratitude for all the love, support, and encouragement from the community, especially our students who have regretfully been criticized for their speaking up in this matter.
<v James Whitfield>大家好，我是James Whitfield博士。Ryan博士，各位董事会成员，首先我想对社区表达我的感激，感谢你们的爱、支持和鼓励，特别是我们的学生，他们因为在此事上发声而遗憾地受到了批评。

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:32.010
<v James Whitfield>I stand before you today no different than I was when I came in in '18-'19.
<v James Whitfield>我今天站在这里，与我在18-19学年刚入职时没有任何不同。

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:36.720
<v Emanuele Berry>The 2018-2019 school year, when he was first hired by the district.
<v Emanuele Berry>2018-2019学年，那是他最初被学区聘用的时候。

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:50.790
<v James Whitfield>I'm an advocate for all kids. I believe every student, regardless of race, religion, sexual orientation, whatever bucket you want to put them in, I believe they all have access to-- they should have access to excellent, equitable education. Yes, I said those words.
<v James Whitfield>我是所有孩子的倡导者。我相信每一位学生，无论种族、宗教、性取向，无论你想把他们归入哪一类，我都相信他们都能获得——他们应该获得优秀、公平的教育。是的，我说了这些话。

00:06:50.790 --> 00:07:17.380
<v James Whitfield>Unfortunately, my unapologetic stance for those things has brought us here tonight, which is disheartening. The attacks from people outside is one thing, but the outright silence and direct actions taken towards me by GSD leadership team-- GCIC leadership team, sorry, I'm trying to get this in-- was absolutely heartbreaking. I can assure you, I have not changed. I'm still the same man today as when you hired me in '18-'19. You promoted me twice in three years. So I ask you what has changed since July 26--
<v James Whitfield>不幸的是，我对这些事物的毫不妥协的立场让我们今晚走到了这一步，这令人沮丧。外界的攻击是一回事，但GCIC领导团队——抱歉，我正在努力说出来——对我的彻底沉默和直接行动让我感到无比心碎。我可以向你们保证，我没有改变。我和你们在18-19年聘用我时是同一个人。你们三年内提拔了我两次。所以我问你们，自7月26日以来发生了什么变化——

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:17.640
<v Board Member 2>Thank you, Dr. Whitfield.
<v Board Member 2>谢谢你，Whitfield博士。

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.220
<v James Whitfield>Dr. Ryan.
<v James Whitfield>Ryan博士。

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:21.820
<v James Whitfield>[SCATTERED CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]
<v James Whitfield>[零星的欢呼和掌声]

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:23.750
<v Board Member 1>Excuse me, please. Sir--
<v Board Member 1>打扰一下，请。先生……

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:24.930
<v Board Member 2>Thank you, Dr. Whitfield.
<v Board Member 2>谢谢你，Whitfield博士。

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:54.210
<v Emanuele Berry>The board's response, they vote not to renew his contract. I reached out to the district, and they said they were not going to talk about this. But in public statements they made earlier, they said the decision was not based on people calling for Dr. Whitfield to be fired. They list a bunch of other reasons, stuff like insubordination. A Facebook post he wrote defending himself was not OK with them, against professional conduct. He talked to the media instead of filing formal complaints with the school.
<v Emanuele Berry>董事会的决定是投票决定不续签他的合同。我联系了学区，他们表示对此事不予置评。但在早些时候发表的公开声明中，他们称该决定并非基于人们要求解雇Whitfield博士。他们列举了一堆其他原因，比如抗命。他为了自辩写的一篇Facebook帖子让他们觉得不妥，认为违反了职业操守。他向媒体谈论了此事，而不是向学校提交正式投诉。

00:07:54.210 --> 00:08:00.830
<v Emanuele Berry>As they read this list at the meeting, you could hear students and parents who came to support Whitfield scoff at the items being read.
<v Emanuele Berry>当他们在会上宣读这份清单时，你可以听到支持Whitfield的学生和家长对所读的内容发出冷笑。

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:13.280
<v Board Member 3>The fifth reason for the recommendation, Dr. Whitfield has diminished his effectiveness by dividing large sections of the community by continuing to raise--
<v Board Member 3>推荐的第五个理由是，Whitfield博士通过继续提出……分裂了大部分社区，从而降低了他的效力……

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:14.490
<v Board Member 3>[LAUGHTER]
<v Board Member 3>[笑声]

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:14.790
<v Man 3>Come on.
<v Man 3>算了吧。

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:15.410
<v Man 4>Are you kidding?
<v Man 4>你在开玩笑吗？

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:15.880
<v Man 5>Wow.
<v Man 5>哇。

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:17.260
<v Man 6>Wow.
<v Man 6>哇。

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:18.990
<v Board Member 1>Please stay quiet.
<v Board Member 1>请保持安静。

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:22.730
<v Emanuele Berry>The response went on for so long she never got to finish the statement.
<v Emanuele Berry>这种反应持续了太久，她甚至没能读完这份声明。

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:23.490
<v Board Member 1>Please be quiet.
<v Board Member 1>请安静。

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:56.890
<v Emanuele Berry>It said he was dividing large sections of the community by, quote, "continuing to raise issues of critical race theory." Doctor Whitfield lost his job. What changed over the last year? As Whitfield said, not him. That email didn't change. The black squares are gone from Instagram. The random reparations money from friends, gone. We went from anti-racist books crowding the bestsellers list to banning kids' books about Rosa Parks.
<v Emanuele Berry>文中称，他通过“继续提出批判性种族理论的问题”而分裂了大部分社区。Whitfield博士失去了工作。过去一年发生了什么变化？正如Whitfield所说，不是他。那封邮件没变。Instagram上的黑色方块不见了。朋友们随机提供的赔偿金没了。我们从反种族主义书籍挤满畅销书榜单，变成了禁掉关于罗莎·帕克斯的童书。

00:08:56.890 --> 00:09:11.830
<v Emanuele Berry>For Dr. Whitfield, the consequence of getting caught in this backlash is he's no longer in the one place he really wants to be. When I spoke with him in early November, he told me he was still dropping his kid off at elementary school. The high school is across the street.
<v Emanuele Berry>对Whitfield博士来说，陷入这场反弹的后果是他无法再留在那个他真正想待的地方了。11月初我与他交谈时，他说他仍在送孩子去小学。高中就在马路对面。

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:34.910
<v James Whitfield>Oh, I mean, I look over there every-- I can't just not look, right? My wife is just like, well, just try to look the other way. And I can't. You know, as I'm driving by, you can see down one of the main hallways, right? There's these big windows, and you can see right down the hallway.
<v James Whitfield>哦，我的意思是，我每次……我无法不去看不看，对吧？我妻子总是说，好吧，试着往别处看。但我做不到。你知道，当我开车经过时，你可以看到其中一条主要走廊，对吧？那里有很大的窗户，可以直接看进走廊里。

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:55.040
<v James Whitfield>And so, as I'm walking by, I'm envisioning the different classrooms and the teachers that I would check on every morning. And it's three stories, and so by the time the first bell had rung, I was already at 10,000 steps because I'm running around this place, checking in on teachers. And so, I think about that every morning.
<v James Whitfield>所以，当我走过时，我脑海中会浮现出我每天早上都会检查的那些教室和老师。那是三层楼高的建筑，所以当第一声铃响时，我已经走了10,000步了，因为我在那里跑来跑去，查看老师们的情况。所以，我每天早上都会想到这一点。

00:09:55.040 --> 00:10:41.090
<v James Whitfield>And so, yeah, it's really hard to pass by a place that you love and know that there's staff members that you love in there. There's students that you love in there. And you know that's where you're supposed to be. But you're not allowed to be in there, right? It's like, what kind of person is not allowed to be in there? You know, it's disheartening.
<v James Whitfield>所以，是的，要经过一个你热爱的、并且知道里面有你热爱的员工的地方，真的很艰难。那里有你热爱的学生。你知道那才是你应该待的地方。但你不被允许进入，对吧？这就像是，什么样的人是不被允许待在那里的呢？你知道，这真令人沮丧。

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:56.830
<v Emanuele Berry>The line of what's acceptable to say about race and racism in America, it moved. It's as though we were having one argument, and then the terms changed. And that shift has left many Black people exposed and vulnerable and living with those consequences.
<v Emanuele Berry>关于美国种族和种族主义的言论底线，它变了。就好像我们原本在进行一场争论，然后规则变了。而那种转变使许多黑人处于暴露和脆弱的境地，并不得不承受这些后果。

00:10:56.830 --> 00:11:12.270
<v Emanuele Berry>This backlash, it's not surprising. This is what America does, Reconstruction, then Jim Crow, the Civil Rights movement, to the war on drugs, Obama to Trump. So no, it's not surprising that there is backlash.
<v Emanuele Berry>这种反弹并不令人惊讶。这就是美国所做的：重建时期，然后是吉姆·克劳法，民权运动，毒品战争，奥巴马到特朗普。所以不，有反弹并不令人惊讶。

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:29.760
<v Emanuele Berry>But what I am surprised by is the way people have been caught up and tangled in it, the choices they've made to either further twist themselves along the line of what's acceptable or move away from it, the way Black people have had to reconsider what to say, and the fallout that comes with those choices.
<v Emanuele Berry>但我感到惊讶的是人们被卷入其中的方式，他们为了让自己在可接受的范围内进一步扭曲，或者试图远离这一事实而做出的选择，黑人不得不重新考虑该说些什么，以及这些选择所带来的后果。

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:47.430
<v Emanuele Berry>Our show today, two stories of people trying to figure out what to say or if they should say anything in this moment of backlash. Stay with us.
<v Emanuele Berry>我们今天的节目，讲述了两个人在这种反弹时刻试图弄清楚该说什么，或者是否应该说什么的故事。请继续关注。

00:11:47.430 --> 00:12:12.630
<v Emanuele Berry>Act 1, Incident. The place that this backlash is playing out most dramatically is in schools, in particular, school board meetings. So I spent a lot of time watching heated school board meetings this year. Sometimes parents are angry about something as small as the email Dr. Whitfield wrote, or a specific book they don't like, or an effort to diversify a library's collection. Sometimes it's a new resolution to promote equity in the school.
<v Emanuele Berry>第一幕，事件。这种反弹最戏剧性地发生在学校，特别是学校董事会会议上。所以我今年花了很多时间观看激烈的校董会会议。有时家长们对像Whitfield博士写的那封邮件这样的小事，或者他们不喜欢的一本书，或者图书馆多元化馆藏的努力感到愤怒。有时是关于在学校促进公平的新决议。

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:31.170
<v Emanuele Berry>And a lot of these parents repeat the same talking points in these meetings across the country. They say microaggressions are not real. What about reverse racism? And somehow, Martin Luther King, Jr. comes up a lot. But what these examples boil down to is we shouldn't be talking about racism because it's not a thing anymore.
<v Emanuele Berry>这些家长中有很多在全国各地的会议上重复着同样的论点。他们说微歧视不是真的。那“反向种族歧视”呢？不知何故，马丁·路德·金总是被提及。但这些例子归根结底就是我们不应该谈论种族主义，因为它已经不存在了。

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:42.130
<v Man 7>First thing I want to say, and it's a fact that Traverse City is not a racist city. And the US is definitely not a racist nation.
<v Man 7>首先我想说的是，特拉弗斯城不是一个种族主义城市，这是一个事实。而美国绝对不是一个种族主义国家。

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:44.380
<v Emanuele Berry>This is from Traverse City, Michigan.
<v Emanuele Berry>这是来自密歇根州特拉弗斯城。

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:56.410
<v Woman 2>This thing about this racism, we have been marching away from racism by leaps and bounds for decades upon decades.
<v Woman 2>关于种族主义这件事，几十年来我们一直在跨越式地远离种族主义。

00:12:56.410 --> 00:13:20.110
<v Emanuele Berry>I keep wondering what is it like to be a kid in one of these towns, for kids of color, especially, when some adults are saying racism is a problem and other adults are saying it's not one at all. For instance, the meeting you just heard from Traverse City, what's so remarkable about this particular meeting is that what these parents are partially responding to is a clearly racist incident in their schools.
<v Emanuele Berry>我一直想知道，在这些城镇里，作为一个孩子，特别是对于有色人种的孩子来说，当一些成年人说种族主义是个问题，而另一些成年人却说根本不是问题时，那是什么感觉。例如，你刚才听到的那场特拉弗斯城的会议，这场特定会议最引人注目的是，这些家长们部分是在回应学校里一起明显的种族主义事件。

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:40.130
<v Emanuele Berry>I've been talking to a kid who was targeted in that incident, a 16-year-old Black biracial girl named Nevaeh, about what had happened to her. And her story is so much more personal and immediate than what you get from any of these meetings. A quick warning, this story might not be appropriate for kids. Here it is, Act 1.
<v Emanuele Berry>我一直在和那个事件中的目标人物交谈，一个16岁的黑人混血女孩，名叫Nevaeh，谈论发生在她身上的事。她的故事比你在这些会议上听到的任何东西都要个人化、更直接。一个小警告，这个故事可能不适合儿童。这就是第一幕。

00:13:40.130 --> 00:14:03.440
<v Emanuele Berry>A year ago, Nevaeh was a sophomore in high school who didn't think about her Blackness too much. Yes, she lives in a mostly white town, 90% white. And yes, she's often the only person of color in a given room, including her family. She's adopted. They're white. But her journals were not filled with tragic Black-girl-in-white-town clichés. She was writing fiction, fantasy, young adult, romance.
<v Emanuele Berry>一年前，Nevaeh是一名高中二年级学生，她并没有过多考虑自己的黑人身份。是的，她住在一个大部分是白人的城镇，90%是白人。是的，她通常是房间里唯一的有色人种，包括在家里。她是领养的，养父母是白人。但她的日记里并没有填满悲惨的“白人小镇里的黑人女孩”的陈词滥调。她在写小说，奇幻、青少年读物、浪漫小说。

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:18.140
<v Nevaeh>I love fiction writing more than anything. I could not write poetry or nonfiction. And then, I'm also really interested in psychology. I've taken that class for-- I'm on my second year taking it now. I find it really interesting.
<v Nevaeh>比起任何东西，我更喜欢写虚构作品。我写不了诗或纪实文学。而且，我对心理学也非常感兴趣。我已经上那门课……我现在是第二年选修了。我觉得它非常有趣。

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:22.750
<v Emanuele Berry>When you say psychology, what is it about psychology that you're interested in?
<v Emanuele Berry>当你提到心理学时，你对心理学的什么感兴趣？

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:34.540
<v Nevaeh>I love learning about why people act the way they do in certain situations. Like, the brain is cool, but I'm more onto, like, the behavioral aspect of psychology
<v Nevaeh>我喜欢了解为什么人们在特定情况下会有那样的行为。比如，大脑很酷，但我更倾向于心理学的行为方面。

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:55.370
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh is a self-proclaimed overthinker. And she does this thing, when you talk with her, that makes her feel both mature and young at the same time. It's that, when she doesn't know something, she says she doesn't know it with such confidence that you feel assured that someday she will know that thing. A year ago, Nevaeh would have described her high school and her experience there as typical.
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh自称是个想得太多的人。当你和她交谈时，她表现出一种既成熟又年轻的感觉。这就是，当她不知道某件事时，她说她不知道时充满了自信，让你确信总有一天她会弄懂那件事。一年前，Nevaeh会形容她的高中生活和她在那里的经历是典型的。

00:14:55.370 --> 00:15:02.030
<v Nevaeh>I mean, like, there are groups here and there. But it's not like the, oh, you can't sit with us, you're not that type of person.
<v Nevaeh>我的意思是，到处都有小圈子。但这不像那种，哦，你不能和我们坐在一起，你不是那类人。

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:03.120
<v Emanuele Berry>It's not Mean Girls situation.
<v Emanuele Berry>这不是《贱女孩》的情况。

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:05.940
<v Nevaeh>No, it's not. It's not like that at all.
<v Nevaeh>不，不是。根本不像那样。

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:17.710
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh had a lot of friends. She wove in and out of a bunch of different groups at school. And then she had her inner circle. One of her closest friends in that circle, someone she talked to daily, I'll call Katie.
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh有很多朋友。她在学校里穿梭于许多不同的圈子中。然后她有自己的核心圈子。那个圈子里她最亲密的朋友之一，一个她每天都交谈的人，我称她为Katie。

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:31.160
<v Nevaeh>We met in seventh grade, and we went to the same school for seventh and eighth grade. And so we were really close. And then ninth grade came along, and I moved schools. But we still kept in contact and we still talked all the time.
<v Nevaeh>我们在七年级认识，七年级和八年级去了同一所学校。所以我们非常亲密。后来到了九年级，我转学了。但我们仍然保持联系，而且我们一直都在交谈。

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:46.100
<v Emanuele Berry>During the pandemic, they would sit on FaceTime together and hang out. Katie was a person who could talk Nevaeh down when she was stressed, especially about school. They were also goofballs together. They'd go to the mall just to try on outrageous outfits and crack each other up.
<v Emanuele Berry>疫情期间，她们会一起坐在FaceTime上聊天。Katie是那种能在Nevaeh有压力时安抚她的人，特别是在学习上。她们在一起时也是活宝。她们会去购物中心试穿离谱的衣服，然后把对方逗得大笑。

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:56.000
<v Emanuele Berry>The racist thing that happened to Nevaeh, the thing that led to those heated school board meetings, that happened in the spring of 2021. And it started with a text from Katie.
<v Emanuele Berry>发生在Nevaeh身上的种族主义事件，那件导致那些激烈校董会会议的事情，发生在2021年春天。它始于Katie发来的一条短信。

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:10.040
<v Nevaeh>And just said that she was in this really, like, messed up group chat with her friends. It was late at night and I was tired, so I didn't really like-- I was like, oh, OK, if it's really that bad, you should leave. I'm going to go to bed.
<v Nevaeh>她只是说她在一个非常糟糕的群聊里，和她的朋友们一起。那时很晚了，我很累，所以我没太……我说，哦，好吧，如果真的那么糟糕，你应该退出。我要去睡觉了。

00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:18.950
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh wasn't really sure what Katie was talking about, so she brushed it off. She woke up the next morning to more messages from Katie about what happened in the group chat.
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh不太确定Katie在说什么，所以她没在意。第二天早上醒来，她收到了更多来自Katie关于那个群聊发生了什么的短信。

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:33.110
<v Nevaeh>She texted me and she was like, hey, you were in it. And I was like, oh, I was in it? What's it about? And then she told me. She was just like, oh, they're, like, bad stuff. They're selling, like, people. And I was like, oh.
<v Nevaeh>她给我发短信说，嘿，你在里面。我说，哦，我在里面？那是关于什么的？然后她告诉了我。她只是说，哦，他们在卖人，之类坏事。我说，哦。

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:54.670
<v Emanuele Berry>The group chat was called Slave Trade, with two purple devil emojis. It's about half a dozen mostly white students listing their Black classmates for sale, posting their pictures and throwing out bids as if they're in an auction, like, a slave auction. Apparently, Nevaeh was one of the people in the chat who was, quote, unquote, "put up for sale."
<v Emanuele Berry>那个群聊的名字叫“奴隶贸易”，后面跟着两个紫色恶魔表情符号。大约有六个大部分是白人的学生，列出了他们的黑人同学进行出售，发布他们的照片并进行竞价，就像在拍卖会上一样，就像奴隶拍卖。显然，Nevaeh是群里被“挂牌出售”的人之一。

00:16:54.670 --> 00:17:05.660
<v Nevaeh>My first reaction was how much did I go for and who did I go to? Full honesty there. And she said that I went to her for free.
<v Nevaeh>我的第一反应是我卖了多少钱，卖给了谁？说实话。她说我被免费卖给了她。

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:23.240
<v Emanuele Berry>The chat is essentially a private text thread that after some time disappears, a feature of the Snapchat app. Some students tell the school district about the chat. The school calls Nevaeh's mom and the parents of other students. And it's while her mom's on the phone with the school that it all starts to sink in.
<v Emanuele Berry>那个群聊本质上是一个私人短信串，过一段时间就会消失，这是Snapchat应用程序的一个功能。一些学生把那个群聊告诉了学校。学校给Nevaeh的妈妈和其他学生的家长打了电话。就在她妈妈和学校通电话时，一切开始显现了。

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:36.440
<v Nevaeh>And at that point, I was like, oh, I was sold. I don't know how bad that is, but I assume it's not the best. And so then I asked my friend to give me screenshots. And she did.
<v Nevaeh>那时，我想，哦，我被卖了。我不知道这有多糟糕，但我认为这肯定不是最好的。所以我就请朋友给我截图。她照做了。

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:57.750
<v Emanuele Berry>This is what they say. First screenshot, one student, "What up, niggas. New group chat for making fun of Black people." In reply, "Very nice." Another screenshot, "All Blacks should die. Let's have another Holocaust." "Yay," someone cheers. "I concur," says another.
<v Emanuele Berry>他们是这样说的。第一张截图，一个学生写道：“嘿，黑鬼们。建立新群聊来嘲笑黑人。”回复：“非常好。”另一张截图，“所有黑人都应该死。让我们再来一次大屠杀。”“耶，”有人欢呼。“我同意，”另一个人说。

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:18.130
<v Emanuele Berry>Picture of a Black kid. "Homo goes for $50. Man's sus. He comes as a bonus. He's free." Someone else adds, "Man so gay, I'll kill him for Jesus." Another screenshot, picture of another Black student, "Negative $10 for autism," someone says. "50% off sale."
<v Emanuele Berry>一张黑人孩子的照片。“那个同性恋卖50美元。那家伙真可疑。他还是个赠品，他是免费的。”另一个人补充说，“那家伙太同性恋了，我会为了耶稣杀了他。”另一张截图，另一名黑人学生的照片，“因为患有自闭症减10美元，”有人说。“五折大甩卖。”

00:18:18.130 --> 00:18:33.060
<v Emanuele Berry>Picture of two black teenagers. Someone says, "They can run, but they can't hide. $100 each, double the trouble. They like picking cotton." Someone makes an offer. "I'll take them for $150 as the pair."
<v Emanuele Berry>两名黑人青少年的照片。有人说，“他们能跑，但躲不掉。每人100美元，双倍麻烦。他们喜欢摘棉花。”有人出价。“我出150美元包下这一对。”

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:48.030
<v Emanuele Berry>Another screenshot, picture of Nevaeh. She's in a red crop top and jeans and a hat, posing near a tree. Written underneath, "Starting bid, $100." "Ew," someone comments. Another "ew." Followed by another.
<v Emanuele Berry>另一张截图，Nevaeh的照片。她穿着红色短款上衣和牛仔裤，戴着帽子，在一棵树旁摆姿势。下面写着，“起拍价100美元。”“呕，”有人评论道。另一个“呕”。接着又是另一个。

00:18:48.030 --> 00:19:15.640
<v Nevaeh>And then just, then and there, everything hit. I was disgusted to the gut. It was just, like, oh, my gosh. I joked with these students before. I couldn't believe that they would think of typing something that they thought like it would be OK. After, like, everything with Black Lives Matter this last year, it was just like, wow, OK. It showed me the type of person they were.
<v Nevaeh>那一刻，一切都击中了心头。我感到胃里一阵翻江倒海。我简直不敢相信他们竟然会想到输入一些他们认为可以接受的东西。在过去一年“黑人的命也是命”运动之后，这简直就像是，哇，好吧。它向我展示了他们是什么样的人。

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:28.620
<v Emanuele Berry>It's almost like Nevaeh's inside the darker, not funny version of that Eddie Murphy skit. Maybe you've seen it. The one where he goes undercover as a white man and discovers all the things white people do when there aren't Black people in the room.
<v Emanuele Berry>这几乎就像Nevaeh处于那个艾迪·墨菲小品中更黑暗、不那么搞笑的版本里。也许你看过那个小品。那个他伪装成白人，发现当没有黑人在房间时白人会做所有事情的小品。

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:47.990
<v Emanuele Berry>She had a new window into who her classmates were in private, in these screenshots. It was the spring of 2021. Luckily, she was in remote school. She wouldn't have to face them until her junior year started in the fall.
<v Emanuele Berry>通过这些截图，她有了新的窗口去了解她的同学们在私底下是什么样的人。那是2021年的春天。幸运的是，她当时在上网课。直到秋季高三开始，她都不必面对他们。

00:19:47.990 --> 00:20:03.660
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh's mom, Jala, saw the screenshots and the words "all Blacks should die" and worried for Nevaeh's safety. She emailed all the school board members, went back and forth with school administrators. She felt like no one was taking it seriously. She called the police.
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh的妈妈Jala看到了截图和“所有黑人都应该死”这些字眼，为Nevaeh的安全感到担忧。她给所有校董会成员发了邮件，并与学校行政人员反复沟通。她觉得没有人认真对待这件事。她报了警。

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:11.020
<v Emanuele Berry>The police investigated the group chat. A few weeks later they told Jala that there were no chargeable offenses. Jala's confused.
<v Emanuele Berry>警方调查了那个群聊。几周后，他们告诉Jala，没有可控诉的违法行为。Jala很困惑。

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:33.670
<v Jala>I said that doesn't make any sense because there was talks of killing all Black kids and starting another Holocaust. To me, that was a direct-- indirect threat of something that could potentially happen. And he just disregarded it as kids being kids and talking smack.
<v Jala>我说那根本说不通，因为里面提到了杀掉所有黑人孩子并开始另一场大屠杀。对我来说，那是潜在可能发生的直接威胁。但他只是把这视为孩子们只是孩子，在说废话。

00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:46.070
<v Emanuele Berry>The school gets back to Jala and Nevaeh about how they will handle the Snapchat incident. Ultimately, they say they will follow the district's policy for discipline, but they couldn't say specifically what that meant.
<v Emanuele Berry>学校回电给Jala和Nevaeh，告知他们将如何处理Snapchat事件。最终，他们表示将遵循学区的纪律政策，但无法具体说明那意味着什么。

00:20:46.070 --> 00:21:08.060
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh was worried. It seemed like the whole thing was just going to be swept under the rug. And she wanted people to know what happened. She hadn't done anything to be a part of the Snapchat except be Black. Should she speak up? But she worried over that too. If I say something, will people think I'm overreacting? But if I don't say anything, will anyone else even know about this?
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh很担心。整件事似乎只是要被扫地出门。她想让人知道发生了什么。除了身为黑人，她什么都没做就卷入了那个Snapchat事件。她应该说出来吗？但她也为此感到烦恼。如果我说出来，人们会觉得我反应过度吗？但如果我不说什么，还有人会知道这件事吗？

00:21:08.060 --> 00:21:23.180
<v Emanuele Berry>In the end, Nevaeh made the decision to speak. She talked to the press about what happened. Instead of disappearing, the story of mostly white high schoolers auctioning off their Black classmates in a secret slave auction, it got a lot of attention.
<v Emanuele Berry>最终，Nevaeh决定发声。她向媒体讲述了发生的事情。这个故事——关于大部分是白人的高中生在一场秘密奴隶拍卖中拍卖他们的黑人同学——不仅没有消失，反而获得了大量的关注。

00:21:23.180 --> 00:21:52.820
<v Emanuele Berry>At first, Nevaeh felt good talking about it. She got a lot of support. People reached out. She felt a sense of relief. But just as quickly, as is happening in many parts of America, talking about racism in Traverse City led to backlash. Nevaeh speaking up set off other, bigger conversations about racism in the community, which led to that heated school board meeting where some adults insisted Traverse City is not a racist place and that any discussion of racism causes division.
<v Emanuele Berry>起初，Nevaeh谈论这件事时感觉很好。她得到了很多支持。人们纷纷联系她。她感到一种解脱。但正如美国许多地方正在发生的那样，谈论特拉弗斯城的种族主义引发了反弹。Nevaeh的发声引发了关于社区内种族主义的其他、更大的对话，这导致了那场激烈的校董会会议，一些成年人坚称特拉弗斯城不是一个种族主义地方，任何关于种族主义的讨论都会导致分裂。

00:21:52.820 --> 00:22:06.290
<v Emanuele Berry>But something that was more important to Nevaeh was the backlash she got from her peers. Some kids started to get tired of seeing Nevaeh on the news. And to Nevaeh, it seemed almost irritated. She noticed a gradual change, even among some of her friends.
