00:32:39.970 --> 00:33:05.770
<v Emanuele Berry>The next day, Nevaeh sent me a text. She made the choice to believe that the blacked out name, the person who did not participate, was Katie. Reading the FOIA made Nevaeh think about their relationship, about who she could trust, something she'd been struggling with. Before this incident, she had no reason to think that Katie wasn't an honest person. She said she reached out to Katie to apologize and Katie had responded.
<v Emanuele Berry>第二天，Nevaeh给我发了条短信。她选择了相信那个被涂黑的名字、那个没有参与的人是Katie。阅读FOIA让Nevaeh开始思考她们的关系，关于她可以信任谁，这曾是她一直挣扎的事情。在这次事件之前，她没有理由认为Katie不是一个诚实的人。她说她联系了Katie道歉，而Katie做出了回应。

00:33:05.770 --> 00:33:26.580
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh did not just want to know what was said about her in the chat. She wanted everyone to know. She wanted to talk about it. But the message she felt like she kept getting from her school, the police, her friends, from the school board meeting in her town, from the 500-page blacked out FOIA, was we don't want to talk about this. We don't want to talk about racism.
<v Emanuele Berry>Nevaeh不仅想知道群里关于她的说法。她想让所有人都知道。她想谈论它。但她感觉从学校、警察、朋友那里，从她所在城镇的校董会会议，从那份500页被涂黑的FOIA中传达的信息是：我们不想谈论这个。我们不想谈论种族主义。

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:39.980
<v Emanuele Berry>It's the same message that has been on repeat everywhere in this moment of backlash. And as a kid, if that's the message you're getting, bury the racism, what do you do with that?
<v Emanuele Berry>在这个反弹时刻，这是无处不在重复的信息。作为一个孩子，如果你得到的信息是“掩埋种族主义”，那你该怎么办？

00:33:39.980 --> 00:34:05.900
<v Emanuele Berry>About an hour after I left their house, Nevaeh's mom sent me a picture, Nevaeh in her room, sitting on her bed, surrounded by the FOIA, which she'd arranged in many little stacks of paper, like a detective sorting and sifting through the clues, still puzzling, still trying to find something to help her make sense of it all.
<v Emanuele Berry>我离开她家大约一小时后，Nevaeh的妈妈发给我一张照片，Nevaeh在房间里，坐在床上，被FOIA文件包围着，她把这些文件整理成许多小堆，像侦探在搜集证据，依然感到困惑，依然试图找到能让她理解这一切的东西。

00:34:05.900 --> 00:34:18.810
<v Emanuele Berry>Coming up, a person who does not want to know anything about what's being said behind their back. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Emanuele Berry>接下来，一个不想知道背后被议论什么的人。过一分钟，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:47.100
<v Emanuele Berry>It's This American Life. I'm Emanuele Berry in for Ira Glass. Today's show, "Talking While Black," stories of people caught up in this moment of backlash. We've gone from 2020, companies tweeting out Black Lives Matter, there's all these diversity initiatives, to now, where it feels like even just saying something is Black is controversial. States are passing laws to limit conversations about racism. Black people are losing their jobs for talking about race too much.
<v Emanuele Berry>这里是《美国生活》。我是Emanuele Berry，代班Ira Glass。今天的节目，“黑人谈话”，讲述了在这个反弹时刻被卷入的人们的故事。我们已经从2020年企业发布推文支持“黑人的命也是命”、充满多元化倡议的时期，到了现在——感觉连说“某些事物是黑人的”都成了争议。各州正在立法限制关于种族主义的谈论。黑人因为谈论种族太多而失去了工作。

00:34:47.100 --> 00:35:03.230
<v Emanuele Berry>I keep returning to this question over the last few months. In this backlash, what are you allowed to say? Like, as a Black person, what about Black lives or the Black experience is actually OK to talk about? This next story gets at this question.
<v Emanuele Berry>过去几个月里，我不断回到这个问题。在这种反弹中，你被允许谈论什么？作为一名黑人，关于黑人生活或黑人经历，究竟什么是可以谈论的？下一个故事触及了这个问题。

00:35:03.230 --> 00:35:26.520
<v Emanuele Berry>We've arrived at Act 2 of our program, Act 2, The Farce Awakens. One place where you can see this dizzying whiplash is books by Black people and about Black people. After the murder of George Floyd, sales of Black books skyrocketed. Ibram X. Kendi's Stamped from the Beginning, The New Jim Crow, So You Want to Talk About Race, were all of a sudden bestsellers.
<v Emanuele Berry>我们进入节目的第二幕，“闹剧觉醒”。你可以看到这种令人眩晕的反弹的一个领域是黑人撰写以及关于黑人的书籍。乔治·弗洛伊德被谋杀后，黑人书籍的销量飙升。Ibram X. Kendi的《从一开始就打上烙印》、《新种族隔离》、《所以你想谈论种族》，一时间都成了畅销书。

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:42.630
<v Emanuele Berry>For a window of time, people really wanted to hear what Black authors had to say. And then, they dramatically did not want to hear anymore. Not only did sales slow, but there are now efforts to ban many of these books that were so celebrated a little over a year ago.
<v Emanuele Berry>有一段时间，人们真心想听到黑人作者要说些什么。然后，他们突然不想再听了。不仅销量放缓，而且现在还有努力要禁掉许多在一年前还备受推崇的书籍。

00:35:42.630 --> 00:35:52.130
<v Emanuele Berry>Producer Chana Joffe-Walt spoke with one author who found his book banned and whose professional arc is sort of a mirror for this backlash. Here's Chana.
<v Emanuele Berry>制作人Chana Joffe-Walt采访了一位发现自己的书被禁，且其职业轨迹也某种程度上是这次反弹写照的作者。这是Chana。

00:35:52.130 --> 00:36:04.980
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I learned about this particular banned book from Deborah Caldwell-Stone. She's the director of the American Library Association's Office for Intellectual Freedom. Her job is to track efforts to censor and ban books.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我是从Deborah Caldwell-Stone那里了解到这本特定的被禁书籍的。她是美国图书馆协会知识自由办公室的主任。她的工作是追踪审查和禁书的努力。

00:36:04.980 --> 00:36:29.400
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And she was telling me she's never seen anything like this fall before, in her decades on the job, just the number of books being challenged, removed from school library shelves. Many books about LGBTQ people, she says those have been contested for years, but more recently, Toni Morrison, Dr. Ibram X. Kendi, The Hate U Give, a novel about the aftermath of police brutality.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她告诉我，在她几十年的工作中，她从未见过像今年秋天这样——挑战、从学校图书馆下架书籍的数量之多。许多关于LGBTQ人群的书籍，她说那些书多年来一直受到争议，但最近，Toni Morrison、Ibram X. Kendi博士、《仇恨的代价》（一部关于警察暴力后果的小说）也被盯上了。

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:42.090
<v Deborah Caldwell-stone>And in Texas there was a mother who halted an author presentation by Jerry Craft by claiming falsely that New Kid represented critical race theory.
<v Deborah Caldwell-stone>在德克萨斯州，有一位母亲通过虚假声称《新来的孩子》（New Kid）代表了批判性种族理论，从而叫停了Jerry Craft的作者讲座。

00:36:42.090 --> 00:36:46.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>New Kid, the, like, comic book, the graphic novel?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>《新来的孩子》，那本漫画书，那本图画小说？

00:36:46.850 --> 00:36:48.530
<v Deborah Caldwell-stone>Yes, New Kid.
<v Deborah Caldwell-stone>是的，《新来的孩子》。

00:36:48.530 --> 00:36:54.540
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>New Kid is a book my children read. It's about a 12-year-old Black boy who goes to a new school.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>《新来的孩子》是我孩子读的书。它是关于一个12岁的黑人男孩去了一所新学校的故事。

00:36:54.540 --> 00:37:14.100
<v Deborah Caldwell-stone>Jerry was scheduled to give an author presentation to the kids in Katy, Texas. And they lined up this big event. All the kids were anticipating meeting Jerry via Zoom. And one mother stopped the whole thing by claiming that it was critical race theory.
<v Deborah Caldwell-stone>Jerry原定要在德克萨斯州凯蒂市为孩子们举办作者讲座。他们排满了这个盛大的活动。所有的孩子都在期待通过Zoom见到Jerry。而一位母亲声称它是批判性种族理论，从而阻止了整个活动。

00:37:14.100 --> 00:37:15.030
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Wow.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>哇。

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:42.930
<v Emanuele Berry>Jerry Craft has been invited to speak to thousands of schools over the years to do author presentations like the one in Katy, Texas, because New Kid was widely acclaimed. It won a ton of awards. It was a number one New York Times bestseller, translated into a dozen languages. New Kid is a semi-autobiographical graphic novel about a boy, Jordan, who wants to be an artist and go to art school. But his parents put him in a fancy private school.
<v Emanuele Berry>多年来，Jerry Craft受邀到成千上万所学校进行作者讲座，就像在德克萨斯州凯蒂市的那次一样，因为《新来的孩子》广受好评。它赢得了大量奖项。它是《纽约时报》排名第一的畅销书，被翻译成十几种语言。《新来的孩子》是一部半自传性质的图画小说，讲述了一个男孩Jordan的故事，他想成为一名艺术家，进入艺术学校。但他的父母把他送进了一所高档的私立学校。

00:37:42.930 --> 00:38:08.100
<v Emanuele Berry>Jordan is one of only a few Black kids. A lot of the kids are wealthy. The campus is vast. And you watch Jordan, this funny, earnest kid, navigate a new environment. There's culture shock. There's new friendships. It's part fish-out-of-water story and part just kid in middle school figuring out crushes in the cafeteria and things like should he do sports or the musical.
<v Emanuele Berry>Jordan是仅有的几名黑人学生之一。许多孩子很富有。校园非常广阔。你看着Jordan，这个有趣、真诚的孩子，在这个新环境中导航。这里有文化冲击。有新的友谊。它既是“局外人”的故事，也是关于在中学食堂里经历暗恋，以及诸如他应该参加体育运动还是音乐剧之类事情的孩子的故事。

00:38:08.100 --> 00:38:22.380
<v Emanuele Berry>Jerry Craft told me when he put new kid out there, the response was amazing. Everything, it was all like a great big hug from the world. People wrote him to say, my kid wants to be an artist now. My kid loved your book. Or--
<v Emanuele Berry>Jerry Craft告诉我，当他推出《新来的孩子》时，反响非常惊人。一切，就像来自世界的一个大大的拥抱。人们写信告诉他，我的孩子现在想成为一名艺术家了。我的孩子喜欢你的书。或者——

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:27.750
<v Jerry Craft>Hey, you know, my kid has never been a reader before and he read your book three times. Like, that's what I'm used to.
<v Jerry Craft>嘿，你知道，我的孩子以前从不读书，他读了你的书三遍。是的，这是我习惯的。

00:38:27.750 --> 00:38:29.340
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Mm-hmm. Love--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>嗯哼。爱——

00:38:29.340 --> 00:38:30.000
<v Jerry Craft>Right!
<v Jerry Craft>对！

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:31.010
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You're used to love and admiration.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你习惯了爱和钦佩。

00:38:31.010 --> 00:38:38.680
<v Jerry Craft>Right. So now it's like, hey, can you comment here in the BBC? I'm like, no. Like, what is this?
<v Jerry Craft>对。所以现在就像是，嘿，你能评论一下BBC的这个吗？我说，不。比如，这是什么？

00:38:38.680 --> 00:39:10.820
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is not his thing, controversy. Jerry was a kid who loved to draw, who became an adult who kept drawing. He spends most of his time in a room by himself making pictures. When he published New Kid in 2019, Jerry had been writing comics for decades. This was the book he'd always wanted to write, the book he wished he'd had as a kid, with a Black character who seemed just regular. Jerry says he remembers going to the library as a kid and it was always the general books and the Black books.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这不是他擅长的领域，争议。Jerry是个热爱画画的孩子，后来成长为一个一直画画的成年人。他大部分时间都一个人待在房间里画画。2019年出版《新来的孩子》时，Jerry已经写了数十年的漫画了。这是他一直想写的书，他希望自己在孩子时能读到的那种书，书中的黑人角色看起来很普通。Jerry说他记得自己小时候去图书馆，总是只有一般书籍和黑人书籍。

00:39:10.820 --> 00:39:28.950
<v Jerry Craft>The general books are "wants to live better." And our books are "just wants to live." You know, your books, "His father is king." Our books, "His father is gone." Lives in a magical kingdom, lives in the hood. All the books with Black protagonists were history or misery.
<v Jerry Craft>一般书籍是“想活得更好”。而我们的书是“只是想活着”。你知道，你的书是“他父亲是国王”。我们的书是“他父亲走了”。生活在魔法王国，生活在街区。所有带有黑人主角的书都是关于历史或苦难的。

00:39:28.950 --> 00:39:59.300
<v Jerry Craft>No kid like me ever made it to the end of a book. There was no dad. There was nothing-- the kids always lived on the south side of somewhere that was just really horrible. And it was gangs or slavery or civil rights or police brutality. And I'm like, can I just see a kid who the biggest problem of the day is whether he wants to play Xbox or PlayStation? And that's why I do what I do. I make those books that I wish I had.
<v Jerry Craft>没有像我这样的孩子能读完一本书。没有爸爸。什么都没有——孩子们总是生活在某个真的很糟糕的地方的南区。要么是帮派，要么是奴隶制，要么是民权或警察暴力。我说，我能只看到一个最大的麻烦就是决定是想玩Xbox还是PlayStation的孩子吗？这就是我做这件事的原因。我创作那些我希望自己曾拥有的书。

00:39:59.300 --> 00:40:25.880
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That's the thing about New Kid being banned. So many of the other Black authors with banned books are writing explicitly about racism or history, books about how slavery has shaped America, police brutality, how to be an anti-racist. But New Kid, this is not a history book. It's not about police violence or slavery or civil rights. It's a story about finding your place in middle school.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这就是《新来的孩子》被禁的事情。许多其他被禁书籍的黑人作者是在明确地写关于种族主义或历史、关于奴隶制如何塑造了美国、警察暴力、如何成为一名反种族主义者的书。但《新来的孩子》，这不是一本历史书。它不是关于警察暴力、奴隶制或民权的。它是关于在中学找到自己位置的故事。

00:40:25.880 --> 00:40:49.460
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>There are racial slights in a sort of daily way. White classmates and teachers make assumptions about where Jordan's from, that he's a great athlete. They say ignorant things, try to touch a Black friend's hair. Administrators continue to call a long-standing Black teacher "Coach," even though he's never coached anything. Jordan often finds this stuff funny. He laughs about it with another Black classmate.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>书中有一些日常式的微歧视。白人同学和老师对Jordan来自哪里做出了假设，假设他是个伟大的运动员。他们说无知的话，试着触摸他黑人朋友的头发。行政人员继续称一名资深的黑人教师为“教练”，尽管他从没教过任何运动。Jordan通常觉得这些东西很有趣。他和另一个黑人同学一起笑话这些。

00:40:49.460 --> 00:41:18.980
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Sometimes he finds it confusing or annoying, but it's not tragic. These are just things that happen on the way, alongside lots of other experiences with his white classmates and teachers, including good experiences. Honestly, the most dramatic thing that happens in this story is that Jordan loses his sketchbook. What is there to object to?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>有时他觉得这令人困惑或恼火，但这并不悲惨。这些只是发生在他人生旅途中的事情，与他白人同学和老师的其他许多经历并存，包括好的经历。老实说，这个故事中最具戏剧性的事情是Jordan弄丢了他的素描本。有什么可反对的？

00:41:18.980 --> 00:41:41.790
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When I talked to Jerry, I didn't know the specifics of the objections in Texas. And I kind of expected Jerry to fill me in, but he knew even less than me. He read enough of one article to understand that a white parent had complained about his book, was calling it critical race theory, a term Jerry had to then Google. And he closed the article without finishing it. He had work to do.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我和Jerry交谈时，不知道德克萨斯州反对意见的具体细节。我本以为Jerry会告诉我，但他知道的甚至比我还少。他读了一篇报道的部分内容，理解到是一位白人家长投诉了他的书，称其为批判性种族理论——Jerry后来不得不去谷歌搜索这个词。他没有读完就关掉了报道。他还有工作要做。

00:41:41.790 --> 00:41:48.150
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But friends and strangers kept messaging him. How crazy, your book, banned.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但朋友和陌生人不断给他发信息。太疯狂了，你的书被禁了。

00:41:48.150 --> 00:42:18.190
<v Jerry Craft>So one person sent me the actual school board link. I guess they filmed the school board? And I just refused to watch that because when I'm sitting for 16 hours a day drawing humorous stuff for kids, I have to protect my own brain and my own psyche because I don't want my next book, kids to be like, wow, Mr. Craft used to be funny. Now he's so depressing. So I have to be in a really good mental space for that.
<v Jerry Craft>所以有人发给我校董会的实际链接。我猜他们拍摄了校董会？我拒绝看那个，因为当我每天坐16个小时为孩子们画有趣的东西时，我必须保护我自己的大脑和心灵，因为我不希望我的下一本书，让孩子们觉得，哇，Craft先生以前很风趣。现在他好压抑。所以我必须保持一个非常好的心理状态。

00:42:18.190 --> 00:42:22.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So, you knew. I mean, it sounds like you knew that that was something that you weren't going to want to watch.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以，你知道了。我的意思是，听起来你知道那是不想看的东西。

00:42:22.610 --> 00:42:25.830
<v Jerry Craft>Yeah. I did not want to go down that rabbit hole.
<v Jerry Craft>是的。我不想掉进那个兔子洞。

00:42:25.830 --> 00:42:32.640
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What did you imagine was down that rabbit hole that was scary to you?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你想象那个兔子洞下面有什么让你害怕的东西？

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:56.640
<v Jerry Craft>The complaint that I saw was that I'm teaching kids critical race theory. So the college course that mentions things such as institutional racism and all these really heavy, deep things that they are studying and picking apart in college, I was now breaking that down for fourth and fifth graders apparently. So--
<v Jerry Craft>我看到的投诉是我在教孩子们批判性种族理论。所以，那个提及诸如制度性种族主义等所有这些非常沉重、深刻的事物，在大学里进行学习和剖析的课程，现在显然被我分解给四年级和五年级的孩子了。所以——

00:42:56.640 --> 00:42:57.310
<v Jerry Craft>[RING]
<v Jerry Craft>[铃声]

00:42:57.310 --> 00:43:00.220
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Ooh, sorry. That's not supposed to happen.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>噢，抱歉。那不应该发生。

00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:03.300
<v Jerry Craft>Oh, is that for me? Is that Katy, Texas?
<v Jerry Craft>噢，那是给我的吗？那是德克萨斯州凯蒂市的吗？

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:05.220
<v Jerry Craft>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jerry Craft>[笑声]

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:06.300
<v Jerry Craft>Like, we wanted equal opportunity.
<v Jerry Craft>就像，我们想要平等的机会。

00:43:06.300 --> 00:43:08.070
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Yeah, they wanted to know what you're talking about.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>是的，他们想知道你在说什么。

00:43:08.070 --> 00:43:10.040
<v Jerry Craft>We have to give an opposing viewpoint.
<v Jerry Craft>我们必须提供相反的观点。

00:43:10.040 --> 00:43:15.660
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That's how I do my interviews. I just, like, in the middle, I get a call from the opposing side, and then the two of you duke it out.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这就是我做采访的方式。我就像，中间，我接到对立方的电话，然后你们两个决斗一下。

00:43:15.660 --> 00:43:18.110
<v Jerry Craft>Right, there you go.
<v Jerry Craft>对，这就是了。

00:43:18.110 --> 00:43:43.950
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I did not stage a call with the mom who complained about Jerry Craft's books, but I did talk to her. And I watched her testimony at the Katy school board meetings, the ones Jerry Kraft would not watch. Her name is Bonnie Anderson. She has three kids in the schools. She's very involved in local school politics. She's part of a lawsuit against the school district's mask mandate. And she ran for school board last spring but lost.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我没有安排和那位投诉Jerry Craft书籍的母亲通电话，但我确实和她谈了。我看了她在凯蒂校董会会议上的证词，那些Jerry Kraft不愿看的会议。她的名字是Bonnie Anderson。她有三个孩子在这些学校上学。她非常参与当地学校的政治。她是反对学区口罩强制令诉讼的一部分。去年春天她还竞选了校董会，但输了。

00:43:43.950 --> 00:43:56.220
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Bonnie told me she first encountered Jerry's book when she got a flyer in her kid's backpack announcing the author visit. So she got the books, New Kid, and the follow-up book, Class Act, and read them both herself.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Bonnie告诉我，她第一次接触到Jerry的书是在她孩子的书包里收到一张传单，宣布作者访问活动。所以她拿到了《新来的孩子》和后续的书《班级行动》，并亲自读了这两本。

00:43:56.220 --> 00:43:58.110
<v Bonnie Anderson>I don't know if you've-- have you read these books?
<v Bonnie Anderson>我不知道你是否——你读过这些书吗？

00:43:58.110 --> 00:43:59.560
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Yeah, my kids read them.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>是的，我的孩子们读过。

00:43:59.560 --> 00:44:11.070
<v Bonnie Anderson>OK, OK. [CHUCKLE] I don't even know where to start. So the first thing that I noticed is all of the microaggressions that the author is depicting.
<v Bonnie Anderson>好的，好的。[轻笑] 我甚至不知道从哪儿开始。所以我注意到的第一件事是作者描绘的所有微歧视。

00:44:11.070 --> 00:44:31.980
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Microaggressions, meaning the everyday thoughtless stuff white people in the book say around Black kids. Bonnie worried how reading those depictions might make white kids feel. The district told me there was another parent too, who apparently filed a complaint that New Kid includes vulgarity. But they would not provide that person's name.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>微歧视，意思是书中白人孩子周围的白人所说的日常不经意的话。Bonnie担心阅读这些描绘会让白人孩子有什么感觉。学区告诉我还有另一位家长，显然也提交了一份投诉，称《新来的孩子》包含粗俗内容。但他们没有提供那个人的名字。

00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:53.400
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Bonnie, though, was very public. She cared enough about this to create a petition demanding the district cancel Jerry Craft's event. It got 400 signatures. To Bonnie, the book has a clear agenda. Jerry set out to convince people systemic racism is real and show how privileged and awful white people are.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>不过，Bonnie非常公开。她关心这件事，以至于发起了一份请愿书，要求学区取消Jerry Craft的活动。它获得了400个签名。对Bonnie来说，这本书有一个明确的议程。Jerry着手说服人们系统性种族主义是真实的，并展示白人是多么享有特权和糟糕。

00:44:53.400 --> 00:45:13.920
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>He's dressing it up with funny or dramatic moments that, to her, seem totally over the top, like the scene where the dad gets pulled over by the cops and is super nervous. She says, you really think that happens? Or the white mom who worries her kid's Black friend might take offense if she serves watermelon for a snack. Come on, she says.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他用有趣或戏剧性的时刻包装它，在她看来，那些时刻完全是过火的，比如爸爸被警察拦下，非常紧张的那一幕。她说，你真的认为那种事会发生吗？或者那个担心孩子黑人朋友如果吃西瓜作为零食会觉得被冒犯的白人妈妈。得了吧，她说。

00:45:13.920 --> 00:45:21.100
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>A lot of our conversation was Bonnie questioning parts of the book, and me saying, I think that's based on Jerry's own life.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们谈话的很大一部分是Bonnie质疑书的部分，而我则说，我认为那基于Jerry自己的生活。

00:45:21.100 --> 00:45:33.420
<v Bonnie Anderson>You really think that Jerry Craft went to an all-Jewish school, which he did, and you really think he was given KFC gift certificates? Because he says these are things he went through.
<v Bonnie Anderson>你真的认为Jerry Craft去了一所全犹太学校（他确实去了），而且你真的认为他被赠予了KFC礼券吗？因为他说这些是他经历过的事情。

00:45:33.420 --> 00:45:36.320
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Wait, it's not a Jewish school.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>等等，这不是犹太学校。

00:45:36.320 --> 00:45:38.720
<v Bonnie Anderson>He did. He went to a mostly Jewish school.
<v Bonnie Anderson>他去了。他去了一所主要是犹太人的学校。

00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:41.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>He went to Fieldston, which is not a Jewish school.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他去了Fieldston，那不是一所犹太学校。

00:45:41.610 --> 00:45:41.950
<v Bonnie Anderson>Mm-hmm.
<v Bonnie Anderson>嗯哼。

00:45:41.950 --> 00:45:47.720
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But you're saying you don't believe that that actually happened to him.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但你在说你不相信那真的发生在他身上。

00:45:47.720 --> 00:46:03.030
<v Bonnie Anderson>Regardless, let's just pretend that all the things he wrote about, being called an Oreo, all those things actually happened. Regardless, let's just pretend for a second that all those things happened. Or let's take his word, all those things happened. It doesn't matter.
<v Bonnie Anderson>无论如何，让我们假装他写的所有事情，被叫成Oreo，所有那些事情真的发生了。无论如何，让我们假装一会儿所有那些事情都发生了。或者让我们相信他的话，所有那些事情都发生了。这不重要。

00:46:03.030 --> 00:46:04.590
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Wait? Why doesn't that matter?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>等等？为什么那不重要？

00:46:04.590 --> 00:46:23.310
<v Bonnie Anderson>Because you don't harm future generations of children because you went through a bad experience. You don't poison the minds of my children even if it happened. You do not poison the minds of other kids and make them feel like they have to make concessions for being white.
<v Bonnie Anderson>因为你不能因为你自己经历过糟糕的经历而去伤害下一代孩子。你不能毒害我孩子的思想，即使它真的发生了。你不应该毒害其他孩子的思想，让他们觉得因为是白人就必须做出让步。

00:46:23.310 --> 00:46:33.150
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is an idea that is repeated in a lot of the efforts to ban books. The idea that these books will psychologically harm children, usually white children.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这是一个在许多禁书努力中反复出现的想法。认为这些书籍会在心理上伤害孩子，通常是白人孩子。

00:46:33.150 --> 00:46:39.600
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When you say it poisons the minds of your kids, what's the thing that you're worried is going to happen to your kids reading these books?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当你谈到毒害你孩子的思想时，你担心这些书会对你的孩子造成什么影响？

00:46:39.600 --> 00:47:05.820
<v Bonnie Anderson>So these books teach children the preordained conclusion that white children have wealth, status, and race privilege, while children of color must suffer the racist ignorance of these privileged families. You remember from the books that the white mom does nothing but play yoga all day and the white boy feels neglected. And they live in a mansion and they drive in the Range Rover.
<v Bonnie Anderson>所以这些书教给孩子们一个预定的结论，即白人孩子拥有财富、地位和种族特权，而有色人种孩子必须承受这些特权家庭的种族主义无知。你从书中记得那个白人妈妈除了每天做瑜伽什么都不做，白人男孩感到被忽视。他们住在豪宅里，开着路虎。

00:47:05.820 --> 00:47:23.480
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's a Mercedes, but sure. For Bonnie, these characterizations would be damaging to her kids if they read the books, which they haven't. New Kid is a book that is entirely focused on the perspective of a Black boy, the things he sees and experiences.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>是奔驰，但随你吧。对Bonnie来说，如果她的孩子阅读这些书籍（他们还没读过），这些刻画将是有害的。《新来的孩子》是一本完全集中在黑人男孩视角、他所见所闻的书。

00:47:23.480 --> 00:47:51.530
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>There are white characters. Some of them are mean. Some of them are Jordan's best friends. One of them talks to everyone through sock puppets. But they're peripheral. The story is about Jordan. So I would assume, like with any well-told story, kids reading this book would identify with the main character, since he's the protagonist of the story.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>书中有白人角色。其中一些很刻薄。其中一些是Jordan最好的朋友。其中一个用袜偶和大家交谈。但他们是边缘化的。故事是关于Jordan的。所以我会假设，像任何讲述得好的故事一样，阅读这本书的孩子会认同主角，因为他是故事的主人公。

00:47:51.530 --> 00:48:20.000
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I want to say something here I feel obliged to say, but also feels a little embarrassing for all of us that I have to say it at all. This book, Jerry Craft's book, New Kid, is not critical race theory. Jerry Craft did not create a fictional 12-year-old Black boy, Jordan Banks, to promote a complex legal theory about systemic racism. And although it is a work of fiction, most of what Bonnie objects to in these books did actually happen.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我想在这里说一些我觉得有义务说的话，但也觉得我们不得不说这些话真的很尴尬。这本书，Jerry Craft的书《新来的孩子》，不是批判性种族理论。Jerry Craft并没有创造一个虚构的12岁黑人男孩Jordan Banks来推广关于系统性种族主义的复杂法律理论。尽管它是一部虚构作品，但Bonnie反对的书中大部分内容确实发生了。

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:23.920
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>How much of this is just your direct life experience?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>其中有多少是你的直接生活经历？

00:48:23.920 --> 00:48:40.330
<v Jerry Craft>There's a lot of it. I was the light-skinned African-American kid, born in Harlem, grew up in Washington Heights. The house where Jordan Banks lives is a brownstone. That is literally the house where I was born and spent the first 25 years of my life. My mom and dad did not want me to go to art school.
<v Jerry Craft>有很多。我是肤色较浅的非裔美国孩子，出生在哈林区，在华盛顿高地长大。Jordan Banks住的房子是褐砂石。那字面上就是我出生并度过前25年人生的地方。我爸妈不想让我去艺术学校。

00:48:40.330 --> 00:48:42.230
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But you, like Jordan Banks, wanted to go to art school?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但你，像Jordan Banks一样，想去艺术学校？

00:48:42.230 --> 00:48:51.080
<v Jerry Craft>I wanted to go to art school. So they sent me to a school in Riverdale. And here, they send Jordan Banks to a school called Riverdale Academy Day School, or RAD, for short.
<v Jerry Craft>我想去艺术学校。所以他们把我送到了Riverdale的一所学校。在这里，他们把Jordan Banks送进了一所叫Riverdale Academy Day School（简称RAD）的学校。

00:48:51.080 --> 00:49:02.810
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Jordan Banks felt out of place. Jerry Craft felt out of place. Jordan Banks' classmates all inexplicably wore pink but called it salmon. Same with Jerry Craft.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Jordan Banks感觉格格不入。Jerry Craft感觉格格不入。Jordan Banks的同学们莫明其妙地都穿粉色，但称其为鲑鱼色。Jerry Craft也是。

00:49:02.810 --> 00:49:15.530
<v Jerry Craft>The whole Vineyard Vines thing, that was like the unofficial uniform. So I changed it, in New Kid, to Grapevine Groves. It's like, wow, you kids sure wear a lot of pink here. It's like, no, it's not pink. It's salmon.
<v Jerry Craft>那个Vineyard Vines风，那是非官方制服。所以我在《新来的孩子》中把它改成了Grapevine Groves。就像，哇，你们这些孩子在这里穿这么多粉色。就像，不，不是粉色。是鲑鱼色。

00:49:15.530 --> 00:49:30.740
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And all the things Jordan Banks observes or experiences, a white mom who worries about serving watermelon, a nerve-wracking interaction with a cop, white classmates touching your hair, assuming you like basketball, they happened to Jerry or Jerry's kids.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>以及Jordan Banks观察或经历的所有事情——担心供应西瓜的白人妈妈、与警察紧张的互动、白人同学触摸你的头发、假设你喜欢篮球——它们都发生在了Jerry或Jerry的孩子身上。

00:49:30.740 --> 00:49:36.110
<v Jerry Craft>Being called the wrong name. You know, hey, Deandre. I'm not Deandre. I'm Drew.
<v Jerry Craft>被叫错名字。你知道，嘿，Deandre。我不是Deandre。我是Drew。

00:49:36.110 --> 00:49:37.440
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Like that's the other one?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>那也是其中一个？

00:49:37.440 --> 00:49:39.680
<v Jerry Craft>Uh-huh. Right, that's the other one.
<v Jerry Craft>嗯哼。对，那是另一个。

00:49:39.680 --> 00:49:55.400
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That's what's so interesting to me about this book in particular being kind of drawn into this CRT battle that's supposedly about history. But your book is not a history book. This is literally just you writing down the story of your life.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这就是为什么我发现这本书特别被拉进这场理应是关于历史的CRT之战中非常有趣的原因。但你的书不是历史书。这字面上只是你写下的你生活的故事。

00:49:55.400 --> 00:50:03.280
<v Jerry Craft>Right, yeah. It literally is based on what I actually see. There's nothing that I haven't lived myself.
<v Jerry Craft>对，是的。它确实基于我实际看到的东西。没有什么是我自己没经历过的。

00:50:03.280 --> 00:50:26.650
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>With one exception, those KFC gift certificates? Jerry says he made that up. One other thing Jerry pointed out that really happened to him, the last chapter of New Kid, it's called "The Farce Awakens." It's when Jordan loses his sketchbook, the one he carries everywhere. He's super stressed because it has all his thoughts and drawings from that year. It's sort of like a visual diary.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>除了一点例外，那些KFC礼券？Jerry说那是他编的。Jerry指出的另一件真正发生在他身上的事情，是《新来的孩子》最后一章，它叫“闹剧觉醒”。那是Jordan弄丢了他的素描本的时候，他随身携带的那本。他压力很大，因为里面有他那一年所有的想法和画作。它有点像视觉日记。

00:50:26.650 --> 00:50:32.350
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It turns out his teacher, Ms. Rawle, is holding his sketchbook when he comes into the classroom. She's read it.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>事实证明，他的老师Ms. Rawle在他走进教室时拿着他的素描本。她读了它。

00:50:32.350 --> 00:50:39.490
<v Jerry Craft>And she reads it, and she's like, Jordan, why are you so angry? It's like, I'm not angry.
<v Jerry Craft>她读了它，说：Jordan，你为什么这么生气？就像，我不生气。

00:50:39.490 --> 00:50:42.070
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And the teacher's upset about what's in the notebook.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>而老师对笔记本里的东西感到不安。

00:50:42.070 --> 00:50:55.630
<v Jerry Craft>Right, because he will complain about being called the wrong name or just different things like that, or people touching his friend's hair. And so, she's like, Jordan, you're special. You're here.
<v Jerry Craft>对，因为他会抱怨被叫错名字，或者诸如此类的不同事情，或者人们触摸他朋友的头发。所以，她就像，Jordan，你是特别的。你在这里。

00:50:55.630 --> 00:51:14.920
<v Jerry Craft>And he's like, yeah, but Ms. Rawle, would you take a job at a school in my neighborhood so you can be special? And she kind of gets on him. He's like, so, let me get this right. It's OK for this stuff to happen to me. It's just not OK for me to talk about it. And that sums up this whole thing that's going on now.
<v Jerry Craft>他就像，是的，但Ms. Rawle，你会去我附近的一所学校工作吗，这样你也能变得特别？她有点对他发火。他就像，所以，让我弄清楚这一点。这事发生在我身上是可以的。只是我不可以说出来就不行。这总结了现在正在发生的整件事。

00:51:14.920 --> 00:51:15.640
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Oh.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>噢。

00:51:15.640 --> 00:51:19.980
<v Jerry Craft>Right? There are things based on my life and based on my kids' lives.
<v Jerry Craft>对吧？这些东西基于我的生活和孩子们的生活。

00:51:19.980 --> 00:51:25.540
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Does it feel like a grown-up version of what happens to Jerry Banks in your novel?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这感觉像是发生在你小说里Jerry Banks身上事情的成年版吗？

00:51:25.540 --> 00:51:28.600
<v Jerry Craft>I love how you said Jerry Banks because that is right.
<v Jerry Craft>我喜欢你说Jerry Banks的方式，因为那是对的。

00:51:28.600 --> 00:51:29.140
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Jordan, Jordan Banks.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Jordan，Jordan Banks。

00:51:29.140 --> 00:51:33.670
<v Jerry Craft>No, you're right. It is Jerry Banks. We are one and the same. No, it's exactly the same.
<v Jerry Craft>不，你是对的。它是Jerry Banks。我们是一个人。不，完全一样。

00:51:33.670 --> 00:51:35.320
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Yeah.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>是的。

00:51:35.320 --> 00:51:48.180
<v Jerry Craft>Ms. Rawle found Jordan's sketchbook and said that it's a polemic and that it's angry. And this woman found my book and says it's a polemic and it's angry, and, yeah.
<v Jerry Craft>Ms. Rawle发现了Jordan的素描本，说那是煽动性的，说他很愤怒。而这个女人发现了我的书，说它是煽动性的，说它很愤怒，是的。

00:51:48.180 --> 00:52:27.110
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I went back to that last chapter recently. And what stood out reading it this time, knowing it had been banned, is that even 12-year-old Jordan Banks knows that his account of his own life will not be acceptable to his white teacher and is better kept in the safety of his journal. He knows his teacher won't be able to read it without feeling personally attacked. He knows that what has happened to him will not matter as much as how it makes her feel to read about it. He already knows that even if it's all real, she'd rather he keep it to himself.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我最近回到了最后一章。这次读的时候，知道它被禁了，我感到特别突出的是，即使是12岁的Jordan Banks也知道，他对自己生活的叙述对他白人老师来说是不可接受的，最好保存在日记的安全中。他知道，如果不感到受到人身攻击，他的老师是无法读到它的。他知道，比起读到它会让她有什么感觉，发生在他身上的事情并不重要。他已经知道，即使这一切都是真实的，她也宁愿他保密。

00:52:27.110 --> 00:52:55.920
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Eight states have recently passed legislation restricting the teaching of racism and bias in public schools. Texas is one of them. Nearly 20 more states have introduced or plan to introduce similar legislation. These laws almost never list specific books you can't teach. They talk about feelings. They prohibit teaching any lessons that might make students feel, quote, "discomfort, guilt, or anguish" because of their race.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>八个州最近通过了立法，限制在公立学校教授种族主义和偏见。德克萨斯州是其中之一。近20个州已经引入或计划引入类似的立法。这些法律几乎从不列出你不能教授的具体书籍。他们谈论感受。他们禁止教授任何可能让学生因为种族而感到——引用——“不适、内疚或痛苦”的课程。

00:52:55.920 --> 00:53:13.100
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>After Bonnie's petition and the complaint about New Kid including vulgarity, the district responded, launched an investigation, and said it was using the new Texas law to evaluate the book. It also pulled Jerry's books from the library and called off his author visit.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在Bonnie的请愿和关于《新来的孩子》包含粗俗内容的投诉后，学区做出了回应，发起了调查，并表示正在使用新的德克萨斯州法律来评估这本书。它还将Jerry的书从图书馆下架，并取消了他的作者访问活动。

00:53:13.100 --> 00:53:42.320
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In Pennsylvania, a group of parents got the school board to ban a list of diverse books, including a children's book about Rosa Parks. They worried the books could be used to make white children feel guilty. A group of white moms in Tennessee have demanded a book about Martin Luther King, Jr. and the March on Washington be removed from the curriculum. They also objected to a chapter book called The Story of Ruby Bridges because it makes it seem like white people are bad.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在宾夕法尼亚州，一群家长让校董会禁掉了一系列多元化的书籍，包括一本关于罗莎·帕克斯的童书。他们担心这些书会被用来让白人孩子感到内疚。田纳西州的一群白人妈妈要求从课程中删除一本关于马丁·路德·金和华盛顿游行的书。她们还反对一本叫《鲁比·布里奇斯的故事》的书，因为那让白人看起来很坏。

00:53:42.320 --> 00:53:58.050
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>These are parents who claim reading books about Black experiences or Black history or just a Black kid going to middle school will harm their children. And their desire to protect their kids from discomfort is now enshrined in the law.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这些家长的声称是，阅读关于黑人经历或黑人历史，或者只是黑人孩子上中学的书会伤害他们的孩子。他们保护孩子免受不适的愿望现在已被写入法律。

00:53:58.050 --> 00:54:20.600
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The investigation into New Kid lasted 10 days, at which point, the district announced their findings, no inappropriate material. Children would be allowed to read this book in Katy, Texas. And Jerry's event with the kids was rescheduled. It went fine. Kids asked the same questions they always do. What inspired you? Is there going to be a movie?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>对《新来的孩子》的调查持续了10天，届时学区公布了他们的调查结果：没有不当内容。孩子们将被允许在德克萨斯州凯蒂市阅读这本书。Jerry与孩子们的活动也被重新安排了。进行得很顺利。孩子们问着他们总是问的问题。什么激励了你？会有电影吗？

00:54:20.600 --> 00:54:44.890
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But that same day, the Katy school board had a meeting. Parents showed up to complain that the event was allowed to go forward, that critical race theory was infiltrating the schools. And they demanded a full audit of the whole library, which, just recently, they won. There was one mom who got up to defend New Kid and to say what the book means to her family.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但在同一天，凯蒂校董会召开了一次会议。家长们出席投诉该活动被允许进行，批判性种族理论正在渗透到学校中。他们要求对整个图书馆进行全面审计，就在最近，他们赢了。有一位妈妈站起来为《新来的孩子》辩护，并讲述了这本书对她家庭的意义。

00:54:44.890 --> 00:54:51.890
<v Woman 3>I've never spoken before, so this is about the Jerry Craft book. I'm sure you all know about that.
<v Woman 3>我以前从未发言过，所以这是关于Jerry Craft的书。我相信你们都知道。

00:54:51.890 --> 00:54:59.910
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Her name is Courtney. She's a Black woman standing before a line of board members who all appear to be white. Here's what she had to say.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她的名字是Courtney。她是一位黑人女性，站在一排看起来都是白人的董事会成员面前。这是她要说的话。

00:54:59.910 --> 00:55:18.840
<v Courtney>Even though the books will be placed on shelves again, I am concerned to what led to them being removed. Can you count on your hand and tell me how many people of color you've ever had at your home? If you're having trouble or just can't remember a day or time, please tell me, what do you possibly know about what's wrong with a book you've never lived?
<v Courtney>尽管这些书将再次摆上书架，但我担心的是导致它们被下架的原因。你们能数一数并告诉我你们家里有多少有色人种吗？如果你们数不出来或者想不起某天或某个时间，请告诉我，你们对一本自己从未经历过的书有什么意见？

00:55:18.840 --> 00:55:31.380
<v Courtney>Have you ever went to work for two years and have someone call you Britney every morning? I have because my name is Courtney. You know the correlation if you've read the book, New Kid, by Jerry Craft. Did you read it?
<v Courtney>你们有没有去工作两年，每天早上有人叫你Britney？我有，因为我的名字是Courtney。如果你读过Jerry Craft的《新来的孩子》，你就会知道关联。你读过吗？

00:55:31.380 --> 00:56:02.340
<v Courtney>Have you ever served this great country in the Washington, DC, inauguration of 2000, Korea, 2001, Iraq, 2003, Afghanistan, 2015, Fort Hood, 2016, only to have your 15-year-old twin son call you in the middle of a workday saying he doesn't like his Paytoh high school, and learned that the doctor visits of anxiety and stomach pains for the last 12 months were only due to a white teen calling your son the N-word for the last year of 2018? Well, I have. If you read the book, you'd understand the correlation, once again, of the Oreo joke.
<v Courtney>你有没有为这个伟大的国家服务过，2000年华盛顿特区的就职典礼，韩国2001，伊拉克2003，阿富汗2015，胡德堡2016，结果却在工作日中间接到15岁双胞胎儿子的电话，说他不喜欢他的Paytoh高中，了解到过去12个月的焦虑和胃痛看病，仅仅是因为一个白人少年在2018年的最后一年一直用N字头词汇称呼你的儿子？好吧，我有。如果你读过这本书，你会再次理解Oreo笑话的关联。

00:56:02.340 --> 00:56:22.740
<v Courtney>How dare you, 444 signatures, remove what little representation my culture has to show its reality and literature form on the modern day shelves. I don't applaud you for placing the books back on the shelves. My only hope is that you not allow the unknown and false narratives to sway your judgment on needed representation of us all. Representation does matter.
<v Courtney>你们怎么敢，444个签名，移除我的文化在现代书架上文学形式中仅存的代表性。我不会为你们把书放回书架鼓掌。我唯一的希望是你们不要让未知和错误的叙述左右你们对我们所有人需要的代表性的判断。代表性确实很重要。

00:56:22.740 --> 00:56:38.750
<v Courtney>Please don't thank me for my service. I do it because I love it. Many friends and battles have died and bled for it. As for Katy ISD, do what you should and need to do to uphold the standards of fairness, justice, and representation for this school district so I can thank you for yours. Thank you.
<v Courtney>请不要感谢我的服务。我做是因为我热爱。许多朋友和战友为此而死，为此而流血。至于Katy ISD，做你们应该做和需要做的事，维护本学区公平、正义和代表性的标准，这样我才能感谢你们。谢谢。

00:56:38.750 --> 00:56:42.180
<v Man 8>Thank you. The next speaker is Emily Lewis.
<v Man 8>谢谢。下一位发言者是Emily Lewis。

00:56:42.180 --> 00:56:45.940
<v Man 8>[SCATTERED APPLAUSE]
<v Man 8>[零星的掌声]

00:56:45.940 --> 00:56:50.490
<v Man 9>Mr. President, you seem to have a problem with our country.
<v Man 9>主席先生，你似乎对我们的国家有问题。

00:56:50.490 --> 00:57:09.590
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In so many corners of the country right now, parents, usually white parents, are showing up to meetings just like this to express their fear, to say they don't want their children harmed or feeling uncomfortable. Courtney is talking about harm that is already happening.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>目前在全国的许多角落，家长们，通常是白人家长，正出现在像这样的会议上表达他们的恐惧，说他们不希望他们的孩子受到伤害或感到不适。Courtney谈论的是已经在发生的伤害。

00:57:09.590 --> 00:57:36.320
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And Jerry Craft is already uncomfortable. He wrote a whole book about feeling uncomfortable. He told me you just learn to deal with it. He never had any laws protecting him from discomfort or school boards worried about his discomfort. What he had was the ability to write it down, to talk about it.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>而Jerry Craft已经感到不适了。他写了一整本书关于感到不适的内容。他告诉我你只是学会去处理它。他从未有任何法律保护他免受不适，也没有校董会担心他的不适。他拥有的是把它写下来、谈论它的能力。

00:57:36.320 --> 00:58:07.010
<v Emanuele Berry>Chana Joffe-Walt is one of the producers of our show.
<v Emanuele Berry>Chana Joffe-Walt是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:58:07.010 --> 00:58:40.580
<v Emanuele Berry>Our program was produced today by Robyn Semien and me, Emanuele Berry, and edited by Chana Jaffe-Walt. The people who put together today's show include Bim Adewunmi, Susan Burton, Ben Calhoun, Zoe Chace, Dana Chivvis, Sean Cole, Michael Comite, Andrea López Cruzado, Damian Graef, Seth Lind, Mary Marge Locker, Tobin Low, Lina Misitzis, Michelle Navarro, Stowe Nelson, Katherine Rae Mondo, Alix Spiegel, Laura Starcheski, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, Julie Whitaker, Chloee Weiner, and Diane Wu. Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman, and our senior editor is David Kestenbaum.
<v Emanuele Berry>我们今天的节目由Robyn Semien和我，Emanuele Berry制作，由Chana Jaffe-Walt编辑。参与今天节目制作的人员包括Bim Adewunmi、Susan Burton、Ben Calhoun、Zoe Chace、Dana Chivvis、Sean Cole、Michael Comite、Andrea López Cruzado、Damian Graef、Seth Lind、Mary Marge Locker、Tobin Low、Lina Misitzis、Michelle Navarro、Stowe Nelson、Katherine Rae Mondo、Alix Spiegel、Laura Starcheski、Christopher Swetala、Matt Tierney、Julie Whitaker、Chloee Weiner和Diane Wu。我们的总编辑是Sarah Abdurrahman，高级编辑是David Kestenbaum。

00:58:40.580 --> 00:59:00.320
<v Emanuele Berry>Special thanks today to New Leonard Media of Traverse City, Mark L. Wilson, Nikole Hannah Jones, Orlando Dial, Eve Ewing, Rob Kerr, Sophia Hassen, Seven Forsen, Andrea-Grace Makuna, Damilola Awofisayo, Jewell Coulter, Ami Chum, Michele Togbe, and Gianna Maltbie.
<v Emanuele Berry>今天特别感谢特拉弗斯城的New Leonard Media、Mark L. Wilson、Nikole Hannah Jones、Orlando Dial、Eve Ewing、Rob Kerr、Sophia Hassen、Seven Forsen、Andrea-Grace Makuna、Damilola Awofisayo、Jewell Coulter、Ami Chum、Michele Togbe和Gianna Maltbie。

00:59:00.320 --> 00:59:26.600
<v Emanuele Berry>Our website, ThisAmericanLife.org, where you can stream our archive of over 750 episodes absolutely free. Again, ThisAmericanLife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our boss, Ira Glass. You know, he's instituted this new rule in the office. He gets to take one bite of anyone's lunch. And when I told him that was insane, he got pretty upset.
